    "q-170678": {
      "dom_id": "step-q170678",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "At a soccer game, $60%$ of the spectators are men. It is known that $20%$ of the men in the crowd are over $50,$ while $30%$ of all people in the crowd are over $50.$ If a person over $50$ is picked at random from the crowd, what is the probability that the person will be a man?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $M$ be the event that the randomly selected person is a man, and let $F$ be the event that it is over $50.$ Then, we require $P(M|F),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|F)=\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(F|M)=0.2,$ since $20%$ of the men at the game are over $50.$\n\n- $P(M)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of all the spectators are men.\n\n- $P(F)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of all spectators are over $50.$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|F) & =\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
