    "q-170681": {
      "dom_id": "step-q170681",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Two groups of high school students are competing in a math contest. Group $A$ consists of $5$ girls and $3$ boys, while Group $B$ consists of $2$ girls and $4$ boys. For the first challenge, one group is randomly selected, and one student is randomly selected from that group. If the selected student is a girl, what is the probability that she is from Group $B?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $B$ be the event that Group $B$ is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected student is a girl. Then, we require $P(B|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(B|G)=\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${B}^{′}$ is the event that Group A is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since Group $A$ consists of $5$ girls and $3$ boys and Group $B$ consists of $2$ girls and $4$ boys, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|B)=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(G|{B}^{′})=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\n- Since the group is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=P({B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|G) & =\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{23}{48})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-170681-a",
