    "q-106240": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106240",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who doesn't eat after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't eat after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|N)=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|M)=0.05,\\,P(N|{M}^{′})=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|N) & =\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.05)(0.49)}{(0.05)(0.49)+(0.32)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈13%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
