    "q-106242": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106242",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who eats after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $E$ be the event that a randomly selected person eats after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|E),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|E)=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E)}=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(E|M)=1-0.05=0.95,\\,P(E|{M}^{′})=1-0.32=0.68.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|E) & =\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.49)}{(0.95)(0.49)+(0.68)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈57%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
