    "q-5787": {
      "dom_id": "step-q5787",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $95%$ of those infected and $4%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $10%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is infected? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(D|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(D|T)=\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T|D)P(D)+P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $95%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|D)=0.95,\\,P(T|{D}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $10%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.1,\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.1=0.9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D|T) & =\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T|D)P(D)+P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.1)}{(0.95)(0.1)+(0.04)(0.9)} \\\\  & ≈0.73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
