    "q-106499": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106499",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $90%$ of those infected and $9%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $14%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $90%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $9%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.9=0.1,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.09=0.91.\n$$\n\n- Since only $14%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.14\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.14=0.86.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.91)(0.86)}{(0.1)(0.14)+(0.91)(0.86)} \\\\  & ≈0.9824\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded four decimal places.",
