    "q-42556": {
      "dom_id": "step-q42556",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "A laboratory test for a particular allergy is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $98%$ of allergy sufferers and $4%$ of non-allergic patients tested positive for the allergy. Only $2%$ of the entire population has the allergy. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is an allergy sufferer? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is an allergy sufferer, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(A|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|T)=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of a person who has the allergy  testing positive is $98%,$ and the probability of a  non-allergic person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.98,\\,P(T|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $2%$ of the population suffers the allergy, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.02,\\,P({A}^{′})=1-0.02=0.98.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|T) & =\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.98)(0.02)}{(0.98)(0.02)+(0.04)(0.98)} \\\\  & ≈0.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
