    "q-106498": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106498",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $94%$ of those infected and $7%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $12%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected? Round your answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $94%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $7%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.94=0.06,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.07=0.93.\n$$\n\n- Since only $12%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.12\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.12=0.88.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.93)(0.88)}{(0.93)(0.88)+(0.06)(0.12)} \\\\  & ≈0.9913\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
